WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.034
A plan begins with purpose.

2
00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:03.876
Values guide conduct.

3
00:00:04.077 --> 00:00:06.756
Cedar Crossing values equitable access.

4
00:00:06.956 --> 00:00:11.106
Its vision imagines a community with welcoming places to
learn.

5
00:00:11.306 --> 00:00:18.097
Its mission describes whom it serves and why: connecting
residents with information and learning opportunities.

6
00:00:18.297 --> 00:00:23.970
A goal turns that purpose toward a broad result: improve
access to suitable study space.

7
00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:27.326
An objective makes progress specific enough to assess.

8
00:00:27.526 --> 00:00:38.642
Here is our hypothetical objective: by the end of an
eight-week pilot, at least eighty percent of surveyed
room users will report finding space suited to their
planned activity.

9
00:00:38.842 --> 00:00:44.280
An activity is the work used to pursue the objective,
such as testing a room schedule.

10
00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.968
Buying chairs is also an activity, not proof that access
improved.

11
00:00:49.168 --> 00:00:55.517
The survey needs a clear question, a response count, and
information about people who could not use the room.

12
00:00:55.717 --> 00:00:58.871
Otherwise a positive result may hide a barrier.

13
00:00:59.071 --> 00:01:04.284
Staff and users help shape the plan, while the manager
remains accountable for decisions.

14
00:01:04.484 --> 00:01:11.234
A short pilot can support a longer-term direction
without locking the library into one permanent solution.

